	},
	{
		"id": "http://zotero.org/groups/358366/items/8WPPFLZC",
		"type": "thesis",
		"abstract": "The Ethiopian Orthodox Täwaḥǝdo Church (EOTC) is quickly becoming one of the largest autocephalous churches in the world, yet its theological distinctives are poorly understood even by related ‘Oriental’ Orthodox traditions. Because theōsis (θέωσις; ‘deification’) is recognised as the characteristic unifying theological trait of the overarching Eastern Orthodox branch of Christianity with which the EOTC claims affinity, this study aims to clarify for the first time how notions of theōsis have been understood within the historic Christian faith of Ethiopia. Understandings of theōsis, of course, have been indelibly shaped by the experiential witness of monastic communities. Therefore, this thesis draws upon the rich monastic literature of Ethiopia (and Eritrea) in order to consider the degree to which this non-Greek, pre-Chalcedonian tradition has inherited, incorporated, adapted, synthesised, stewarded, and developed a vital theological concept that was largely (though not exclusively) shaped by Greek-speaking Fathers of the Church. In the process, this study outlines fresh paradigms for conceptualising theōsis that allow the organic approaches of the Ethiopian tradition to be represented without capitulating to categories that have become standard within the broader Orthodox tradition. The findings and translations offered here demonstrate in unprecedented ways how the EOTC has expressed concepts of theōsis and integrated them as their own. This is shown through unprecedented analysis of Gǝʿǝz terminology, phrases, and metaphors, in addition to unprecedented transcription and translation of large portions of Ethiopic monastic literature. Ultimately, this thesis argues that the expressions of theōsis found throughout the monastic tradition of the EOTC represent unique, irreducible insights on the human experience of growth in godliness and are of considerable value for the perpetual theologising tasks of other Orthodox traditions—and ultimately the global Church as a whole.",
		"license": "Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International",
		"note": "dimensions: 3427310 Bytes",
		"publisher": "Anglia Ruskin Research Online (ARRO)",
		"source": "DOI.org (Datacite)",
		"title": "Expressions of <i>theōsis</i> in Selected Ethiopic Monastic Literature",
		"URL": "https://aru.figshare.com/articles/thesis/Expressions_of_i_the_sis_i_in_selected_Ethiopic_monastic_literature/29646740",
		"author": [
			{
				"family": "Samuelson",
				"given": "Calum"
			}
		],
