	{
		"id": "http://zotero.org/groups/358366/items/GPYHI9EX",
		"type": "article-journal",
		"abstract": "The monolithic churches of Lalibela are commonly regarded as evidence for the shift of the Christian kingdom of Ethiopia from Aksum to the Ethiopian Highlands during the thirteenth century AD. Recent research, however, has shown that the rock-cut churches were not created\n              ex nihilo.\n              New archaeological evidence has emerged for an earlier, local troglodytic culture, particularly at Washa Mika'el, further illuminating the cosmopolitan society that existed in medieval Ethiopia. This article considers the role played by this troglodytic culture in the Christianisation of the Ethiopian Highlands and how it attests continuity with its predecessors, especially in the way that sculpted decor are perpetuated and transformed in the frame of a new religious background.",
		"container-title": "Antiquity",
		"DOI": "10.15184/aqy.2021.20",
		"ISSN": "0003-598X, 1745-1744",
		"issue": "380",
		"language": "en",
		"license": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/terms",
		"page": "467-486",
		"source": "DOI.org (Crossref)",
		"title": "The Rock-cut Churches of Lalibela and the Cave Church of Washa Mikaʾel: Troglodytism and the Christianisation of the Ethiopian Highlands",
		"URL": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0003598X2100020X/type/journal_article",
		"volume": "95",
